<p>
Charles Choi has been writing a bunch of nice little apps lately—more than I can cover. His latest, <a href="http://yummymelon.com/devnull/announcing-aqui.html">Aquí</a>, is sort of a niche application but is potentially useful for more than just its intended use.
</p>
<p>
The back story is that Choi really likes the <code>sunrise-sunset</code> command in Emacs that tells him the times that the sun rises and sets. The problem is that <code>sunrise-sunset</code> requires that you first specify the latitude and longitude of where you want to know the times. The standard way of doing that is to record them in your <code>init.el</code>, which is what I do. Choi, however, travels with his laptop but still wants to know when the sun is coming up or going down. There&rsquo;s no automatic way of setting the information so he wrote Aquí to provide it. The only downside is that it works exclusively on the Mac platform.
</p>
<p>
Once installed, all you need do is call <code>aqui</code> to set all the appropriate information. I hardly ever travel these days so I wasn&rsquo;t going to bother but it&rsquo;s easy to use and can give you your current latitude and longitude as well as your address so I thought, &ldquo;Why not?&rdquo;. After I installed and ran it, it gave me my exact address as well as my latitude and longitude. That might sometimes vary a bit depending on the vagaries of GPS but it&rsquo;s certain to be close.
</p>
<p>
If you decide to try it out, be sure to take a look at the <a href="https://kickingvegas.github.io/aqui/index.html">user manual</a> so see what commands are available. Mostly, though, you&rsquo;ll just need to run <code>aqui</code>. Also be sure to check out the installation section because you need to install a shortcut. That&rsquo;s just a matter of clicking on a link in the manual but Aquí won&rsquo;t work until you do.</p>
